  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(xyz),$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $\u0394f$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,3,4).$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y+\\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,1,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,1,1) & =1\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+1\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+\u0394x,1+\u0394y,1+\u0394z),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\u0394f\u2248\u0394x+\u0394y+\u0394z.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,3,4),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394x & =2-1=1, \\\\ \u0394y & =3-1=2 \\\\ \u0394z & =4-1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394f & \u2248\u0394x+\u0394y+\u0394z \\\\  & \u22481+2+3 \\\\  & \u22486.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $6.$",
